{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# rebar's hardcoded Jira vocabularies disagree with a real DC instance in four places\n\n## Provenance\n\nFound by the capability-map job (run **30863672922**, ticket 259b) on its first execution — Jira DC\n8.17.1, Server + Software licensed, a Scrum-template project. Every item below is a direct\ncomparison of a rebar constant against what the instance actually offers, with the raw REST\nrequest/response recorded in that run's `evidence.json`.\n\nNone of these would surface as a test failure today. Three are silent by construction.\n\n## The four defects\n\n**1. Status `IDEA` does not exist.** `LOCAL_STATUS_TO_JIRA['idea'] = 'IDEA'`\n(`jira_family/value_maps.py`), but no status of that name exists in ANY workflow bound to ANY issue\ntype on the instance. Every SCRUM type uses \"Software Simplified Workflow for Project SCRUM\", whose\nonly statuses are To Do / In Progress / Done. **A local `idea` ticket can therefore never sync its\nstatus** — the mapped value matches nothing. Silent: the transition simply never resolves.\n\n**2. Label limit is 254, not 255.** `JIRA_LABEL_MAX_CHARS = 255` (`value_maps.py:58`) is INCLUSIVE,\nbut the instance REJECTS a 255-character label. The effective ceiling is 254. So a label of exactly\n255 chars is fitted to the limit and then rejected by Jira — and per bug 6afc, ACLI exits 0 on that\nclass of rejection, so it fails silently and re-emits. (`JIRA_SUMMARY_MAX_CHARS = 254` was checked\nin the same pass and IS correct.)\n\n**3. Two distinct issue types are both named `Task`** (ids 10003 and 10004). Any name-based type\nresolution is ambiguous and may bind to the wrong one, non-deterministically. rebar's\n`LOCAL_TYPE_TO_JIRA` maps by NAME.\n\n**4. Three link relations are unrepresentable on stock Jira** — `supersedes`, `discovered_from`,\n`caused_by` have no corresponding link type. `RELATION_TO_JIRA_LINK` maps only `blocks`,\n`depends_on` and `relates_to`, so those three silently do not sync. Whether that is intended is not\nrecorded anywhere; it should be a documented decision rather than an accident.\n\n## What the same pass CONFIRMED as correct, so it is not re-litigated\n\nIssue types Bug/Story/Task/Epic all present; all five priorities (Highest..Lowest) present; link\ntypes Blocks and Relates present; **and the link DIRECTIONS verified end-to-end** — `blocks` and\n`depends_on` are true inverses of the single `Blocks` type, `relates_to` symmetric, all three\nconfirmed by creating real links and reading both endpoints. That was the highest-risk vocabulary\n(a direction error inverts a dependency silently) and it is correct.\n\nTransition names also equal their destination status names on this workflow, so bug 7f93's\nname-vs-destination hazard does not currently bite here — but that is a property of THIS workflow,\nnot a guarantee.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [x] each of the four is triaged: fixed, or recorded as an accepted limitation with a reason\n- [x] the `idea` status gap has a decision — map it to an existing status, or state that `idea`\n      tickets deliberately do not sync status\n- [x] the label limit is corrected to the enforced value, with a test that would catch an\n      off-by-one against the instance rather than against a constant\n- [x] the duplicate `Task` name is handled by resolving types by ID where available, or the\n      ambiguity is documented as harmless with evidence\n- [x] the three unmapped relations are documented as deliberate, or mapped\n- [x] the declared environment contract (3fe5) asserts these so a future instance change fails loudly\n\n## Criteria dispositions (operator, 2026-08-04) — Gerrit 1375 + 1381\n\n**AC1 / AC2 / AC5 — MET.** All four findings triaged; see the TRIAGE-2E47-A comment. `idea` is a\ndocumented operator prerequisite (unchanged, deliberately). The unmapped relations are now DATA\n(`UNSYNCED_RELATIONS`, four of them with per-relation reasons) with a test asserting the synced and\nunsynced sets exactly partition `rebar.types.Relation`, so a new relation with no sync decision fails\nloudly instead of silently never syncing.\n\n**AC3 — AMENDED, because the criterion as written prescribes an outcome that is WRONG.** It says to\ncorrect the label limit \"to the enforced value\". Doing that turns four characterization oracles red:\n`sanitize_label` RAISES above the ceiling rather than truncating, so a shared 254 makes the\nlive-validated CLOUD path reject a 255-char label that Cloud accepts — trading a silent DC defect for\na loud Cloud regression, which is the one thing this epic was built to prevent. Same class of\namendment as the epic's own AC5 note (\"prescribes an outcome that would be WRONG if achieved\").\n\nWhat this ticket delivers instead, and what moves:\n* DELIVERED — the TEST half of AC3, which is the part that was actually missing: a live cell posts 254\n  and 255-character labels to the real instance and reads them back, with 254 as a control leg, so the\n  off-by-one is measured against the INSTANCE rather than against a constant agreeing with itself.\n  Unit pins now use literal 255/256 for the same reason.\n* MOVED — the per-deployment ceiling itself, to [rebar:2c93-ce1c-127e-4d58], which also records the two\n  decisions it needs (raise-vs-truncate, and whether Cloud's documentary 255 is even real — if Cloud\n  also rejects 255 the divergence collapses and a shared 254 becomes correct, so that measurement\n  comes first).\n\n**AC4 — the measurement is in place; it needs one harness run to report.** Resolving types by ID is a\nchange to the DC create path, and \"documented as harmless with evidence\" needs evidence I did not have:\nwhether the PROVISIONED PROJECT's issue-type scheme exposes `Task` once or twice. A create posts\n`issuetype: {\"name\": ...}` scoped to a project, so that — not the instance-wide duplicate — is what can\nactually reach rebar. The 3fe5 declared-environment contract now asserts project-scoped issue-type\nname uniqueness and prints the instance-wide entries as evidence (Gerrit 1381). It FAILS provisioning\nif a name is offered twice, which is the condition under which a create could bind non-deterministically.\n\n**AC6 — satisfied by the same contract change**, for the items that belong there: issue-type name\nuniqueness is asserted at provisioning time where a future image change fails loudly and\nattributably, and the label ceiling is asserted against the instance by the live cell."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785881074131407001,"uuid":"8967ea48-347e-47bd-b061-cabad0f755c3"}